hey everyone its Peter Brown reading for  Steve Watts Ori and the blind forest was  a delight in 2015 a tough-as-nails  combination of Metroidvania structure  and meatboy leg demands with a  surprising amount of heartfelt heft moon  studios follow up Ori in the will of the  wisps is every bit as graceful and  lovely as his predecessor even if some  of the emotional beats and exploration  feel a little less novel the second time  around  [Music]  all of the wisps picks up immediately  we're blind forest left off with orys  patchwork family unit welcoming a new  member the outlet coup the family is  happy and loving but who wants to fly  and Ori wants to help her soon the two  are swept off in a gale to a new forest  deep with rot which begins the adventure  in earnest the theme throughout the  story is the encroachment of the decay a  creeping evil that overtook the  neighboring forest after its own magical  tree wither but if it's meant to be ugly  you wouldn't know it from many of the  lush backgrounds especially in the case  of a vibrant underwater section in the  game War II is often swallowed up by  these sweeping environments emphasizing  just how small the little forest Spirit  is compared to their massive  surroundings  [Music]  Hori suite of acrobatic moves makes  delving into new areas a thrilling treat  exploration becomes especially engaging  as you unlock more abilities and become  increasingly adept some of them are  lifted directly from the first game  which can be disappointing next to the  excitement of discovering a shiny new  ability still those old standbys work  well and make the improvisational leaps  and bounds feel as great as ever the  picturesque vistas seem to be pushing  the hardware hard however playing on an  Xbox one X I encountered visual glitches  like screen freezes on a semi-regular  basis and the map would stutter usually  these were simple nuisance but once in a  while it would come mid leap and throw  off my sense of momentum and direction a  day one patch significantly reduces the  freezing and fix the map issue  altogether while Ori is ostensibly a  Metroidvania will of the wisps is less  focused on exploration and backtracking  than is typical for the genre your  objectives are usually clear straight  lines and shortcuts littered throughout  the environments get you back to the  main path quickly most of the Wanderlust  comes in the form of plentiful and  optional side-quests I was glad for the  freedom to pursue a critical path  without artificial barriers but I also  plan to go back and plumb the depths  simply to spend more time in the world  usually the reduced emphasis on  exploration seems to have been replaced  by a major expansion of combat rather  than the passing nuisance of the  occasional enemy all of the wisps  introduces myriad threats that are near  constant presence thankfully the combat  system has been overhauled to match the  elegance of the platforming the story  progresses forward with a sword and bow  with optional weapons for purchase the  combat does take some getting used to  though in part because it's built to  work in conjunction with orys nimble  moves well I felt awkward and imprecise  in combat at the start slashing my sword  wildly even at the mildest of monsters  my comfort level grew as I gained new  platforming skills around the mid game I  realized I had become adept at stringing  together platforming and combat skills  err dashing and bounding between threats  with poetic rhythm and barely touching  the ground until the screen had been  clear that level of finesse is necessary  because Orion will of the wisps  introduces a series of massive boss  battles each more complex than anything  in blind forest their attack patterns  are often signaled by barely perceptible  tells most of the time the boss fills up  a significant portion of the  interactable foreground and even more of  the background but this can make it  frustratingly difficult to tell what is  and isn't vulnerable to your attacks or  what parts will do crash damage this all  makes defeating them feel like a relief  in accomplishment  though sometimes more of the former than  the latter likewise tension-filled  escape sequences dot the map requiring  almost perfect precision and execution  of your toolset to survive a gauntlet of  threats the game offers occasional  checkpoints in these sections as well as  a more generous checkpointing feature  around the overworld the sprawling  bosses and climactic escapes are ways to  express a larger more operatic feel for  will of the wisps blind forest was a  humble little game that told an intimate  relatable fable whisps has a grander  scope and in the process it loses some  of that intimacy it still has moments  with emotional heft both exhilarating  and heartbreaking and Moon Studios still  has a way of expressing an incredible  degree of wordless emotion with subtle  moments of body language the story and  will of the wisps is often darker and  even as touching moments are more  bittersweet the chief antagonist an owl  named shriek is similar to the first  games Kuro and having suffered a tragedy  in the past but how that story addresses  that tragedy is significantly sadder and  stands as a moment of haunting animation  that will stay with me more than any  other single image from the game even  the moments of finality that end the  story while appropriately heroic and  hopeful are tinged with quiet sadness  and inevitability the sense that  everything ends that finality could  signal that this is the last Ori game a  farewell to a fantastical world and  memorable characters that made Moon  Studios such as standout developer from  its very first effort if that is the  case you can hardly ask for a better  send-off Rory and Will O the wisps is a  remarkable synthesis of artful design  and beautiful moments  [Music]
 

